    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1681</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Imre Thököly</a>, reinforced by an Ottoman contingent, is recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1682</span><span class="rf-text">The Ottoman army is mobilised on 21 January and war is declared on 6 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1683</span><span class="rf-text">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> binds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> to mutual support</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1683</span><span class="rf-text">The main Ottoman army reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha">Kara Mustafa</a> demands its surrender</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Early September 1683</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Sobieski</a> cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulln">Tulln</a> to join the imperial troops</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1683</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> occupy the Burg ravelin and the low wall nearby</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1683</span><span class="rf-text">The relief army attacks and defeats the Ottoman army, ending the siege</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1699</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> cedes much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-vienna-1683" data-v="21afc85d55" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Ottoman army attacks before the allies are deployed</b> The Ottoman army attacked early, seeking to disrupt the allied deployment. The imperial troops on the left and in the centre met the attack while the Polish troops were still coming into place.</li><li><b>The imperial army counterattacks in the north</b> The Germans were the first to counterattack. After heavy fighting and several Ottoman counterattacks, the imperial army took the fortified villages of Nussdorf and Heiligenstadt.</li><li><b>Polish infantry advances on the Ottoman right</b> Polish infantry advanced against the Ottoman right flank. Parts of the Ottoman army kept attacking the city, which let the Poles move forward, and by 4:00 pm they held Gersthof.</li><li><b>The imperial left presses on to the Türkenschanze</b> The Germans resumed the offensive on the left at 3:30 pm. They met fierce resistance at first, then advanced by 5:00 pm and took Unterdöbling and Oberdöbling, closing on the central Ottoman position.</li><li><b>Hussars probe the Ottoman line</b> The Polish cavalry came out of the forest, and a detachment of 120 hussars made a probing charge that showed the Ottomans could be attacked, at the cost of many casualties. The vizier left the Türkenschanze for the main camp.</li><li><b>The cavalry charges down the hills</b> The king ordered the cavalry to attack in four contingents. The charge broke the Ottoman lines, the Ottomans retreated, and the horsemen rode for the camps and headquarters while the Viennese garrison sallied out to join in.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha.jpg" alt="Kara Mustafa Pasha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha">Kara Mustafa Pasha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Kara_Mustafa_Pasha,_RP-P-1894-A-18289.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rijksmuseum, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> had long been an Ottoman aim, because the city controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the overland trade routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. In the years before the siege the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha">Kara Mustafa Pasha</a> had roads and bridges repaired and had ammunition and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> sent to depots in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. For 20 years a peace, the Peace of Vasvár, had held between the two empires.</p><p>In Habsburg-held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, unrest had grown into open rebellion against Leopold I's pursuit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> principles and his wish to suppress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. In 1681 Protestants and other anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuruc">Kuruc</a> forces led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Imre Thököly</a> were reinforced by an Ottoman contingent, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> recognised Thököly as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a>. They also promised the Hungarians a Kingdom of Vienna should the city fall. Clashes between Thököly's forces and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> grew in 1681 and 1682, and Habsburg incursions into central Hungary gave Kara Mustafa his argument to persuade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-iv">Mehmed IV</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divan">Divan</a> to allow the army to move. The sultan authorised him to operate as far as Győr and Komárom. The army was mobilised on 21 January 1682 and war was declared on 6 August 1682.</p><p>The 15-month gap between mobilisation and the full invasion gave Vienna time to prepare. Leopold gathered troops and formed an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and Pope Innocent XI. Under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> of 1683 he promised to support John III <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Sobieski</a> if the Ottomans attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, and the Polish army was to relieve Vienna if it were attacked.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski.jpg" alt="Jan III Sobieski" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Jan III Sobieski</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schultz_John_III_Sobieski.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Daniel Schultz / Possibly Andreas Stech, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Figures for the Ottoman side differ. One account puts the army at 28,400 men or approximately 50,000 during the siege, and the whole campaign force at around 90,000, of whom 35,000 were left at Győr and 50,000 went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Kahraman Şakul assesses the Ottoman army as approximately 120,000 soldiers and 156 guns, noting that only the household and retinue troops are counted exactly and the numbers of provincial and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> troops are expected ones. Another estimate has the Austrian ambassador Kunitz giving 180,000 men, a third of them away from the siege. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had 130 field guns and 19 medium-calibre <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. Troops from the vassal states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, Wallachia and Moldavia held bridges on retreat routes and did not take part in the battle.</p><p>Emperor Leopold left Vienna for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> with his court, and Charles V, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, withdrew his 20,000 towards Linz. The city was held by Ernst Rüdiger von Starhemberg with 15,000 troops and 8,700 volunteers, who had 370 cannon.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Sobieski</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 15 August. The Lithuanian army under Kazimierz Jan Sapieha campaigned in the Hungarian Highlands instead and reached Vienna only after it had been relieved. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Imperial troops, joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> troops and others, took Sobieski as the overall commander. One assessment gives the relief army 65,000 soldiers with 165–200 guns, another 70,000–80,000. Mustafa gave the defence of his rear to the Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, whose cavalry numbered between 30,000 and 40,000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The siege began on 14 July, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha">Kara Mustafa</a> sent the traditional demand to surrender, which Starhemberg refused. Food supplies into the city were almost entirely cut. By early September about 5,000 Ottoman sappers had opened gaps about 12 metres wide in the walls, and on 8 September the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took the Burg ravelin and the low wall nearby. The relief army meanwhile crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulln">Tulln</a> on 6 September and signalled its arrival on the Kahlenberg with bonfires.</p><p>At 4:00 am on 12 September the Ottoman army attacked, hoping to disrupt the allied deployment. Charles of Lorraine's imperial army on the left took the fortified villages of Nussdorf and Heiligenstadt after heavy fighting. Kara Mustafa counterattacked with most of his forces but held back some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">Sipahi</a> units for an assault on the city. His sappers had prepared ten mines under the Löbelbastei, but the defenders found and disarmed them. In the early afternoon the Polish infantry advanced on the Ottoman right, and by 4:00 pm the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> held the village of Gersthof. The imperial forces advanced again from 3:30 pm and took Unterdöbling and Oberdöbling by 5:00 pm, closing in on the Türkenschanze.</p><p>At around 6:00 pm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Sobieski</a> ordered the cavalry to attack in four contingents, three Polish and one from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. 18,000 horsemen charged down the hills, Sobieski leading 3,000 Polish heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-hussars">Winged Hussars</a>. The Ottomans, exhausted and demoralised, broke and began to retreat, and the garrison sallied out. Less than three hours after the charge the allied forces had won the battle.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Counts of the Ottoman losses differ. They lost at least 20,000 men during the siege, and in the battle around 15,000 dead according to Podhorodecki, or 8,000–15,000 dead and 5,000–10,000 captured according to Tucker. An Ottoman account captured after the battle lists 48,544 casualties up to 10 September. The relief force lost approximately 3,500 dead and wounded, including 1,300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>; Tucker's estimate is 4,500. The Viennese garrison and the civilian population lost about half their number from all causes during the siege.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had also pillaged the countryside. At Perchtoldsdorf, whose people had barricaded themselves in a church fortress and surrendered on a promise of safety, the men were massacred and the women and children enslaved. In total 57,220 people are recorded as taken away as slaves from the Austrian and Hungarian border zone in 1683: 6,000 men, 11,215 married women, 14,922 unmarried women under 26 and 26,093 children.</p><p>On 25 December <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha">Kara Mustafa Pasha</a> was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> by strangulation, by order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> Agha. The Ottoman historian Silahdar Findiklili Mehmed Agha called the defeat the most disastrous since the foundation of Ottoman statehood in 1299. Among the allies, tension arose over the loot of the Ottoman camp, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Sobieski</a> claimed first choice, and the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> left at once and refused to continue the pursuit. Sobieski liberated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a> after the Battle of Parkany, though dysentery halted his pursuit. Starhemberg ordered the walls repaired at once.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ottoman side is known partly from Ottoman records and partly from Austrian ones, and they differ widely on numbers. An anonymous Ottoman table of siege casualties survives, and Kahraman Şakul argues that only its figures for household and retinue troops are counted, the rest being rounded or expected numbers. Ottoman accounts give the household army as 25,529 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, 3,045 weaponeers and 4,000 gunners, 32,574 in all, against 60,000 in the table. The Austrian ambassador Kunitz reported what he learned from Ottoman captives. An Ottoman account captured after the battle gives the casualties up to 10 September. The contemporary Ottoman historian Silahdar Findiklili Mehmed Agha wrote of the defeat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Sobieski</a>'s own letter to his wife describes the loot.</p><p>Several points are disputed. Whether the relief army numbered 65,000 or more is argued: the figure of 68,000 is said to come from counting a Polish contingent of 3,000 twice. Spencer Tucker's claim that 40,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> arrived east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 7 July has been challenged on the grounds that the Crimean Khanate's population of around 180,000 could not have supported such a force. There is doubt, too, about how far the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> took part in the battle, since their Khan refused to attack the relief force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> crossing and as it left the Vienna Woods. Some historians have speculated that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha">Kara Mustafa</a> declined an all-out assault because he wanted to take the city intact, and not to start the plundering that would follow an assault.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Prince Eugene of Savoy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prinz_Eugene_of_Savoy.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacob van Schuppen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Some historians hold that the battle was a turning point in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-habsburg-wars">Ottoman–Habsburg wars</a>, a struggle of 300 years, and the culmination of 150 years of tension since the failed siege of 1529. It marked the start of military cooperation between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In the war that lasted until 1699 Charles of Lorraine captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1686, established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> control over southern Hungary and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in 1687 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1688. Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, Maximilian II Emanuel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> went on to further conquests. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699 ceded much of Hungary to the Habsburgs. The battle has been called the historic end of Ottoman expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>News of the defeat caused celebrations in Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, where Shah Suleiman I considered a march to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> but did not make one, because officials were aware of the decline of Safavid military strength.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Sobieski</a> is recorded as saying "Venimus, vidimus, Deus vicit", "We came, we saw, God conquered". Pope Innocent XI extended the feast of the Holy Name of Mary to the whole Church in commemoration, and the Austrians built a church on the Kahlenberg. Some have proposed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">Al-Qaeda</a> chose 11 September 2001 for its attacks because of the day's perceived significance in this battle. In 2024 the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> rejected a monument to Sobieski over concerns about Islamophobia and anti-Turkish sentiment.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 12 September 1683 a relief army of Imperial and Polish forces under John III <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Sobieski</a> defeated the Ottoman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha">Kara Mustafa Pasha</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, ending a siege of two months. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> fought on until 1699 and lost most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</em></p>

